WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.160
Before Nintendo became Nintendo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:07.620
it was a card company that began in 1889 by&nbsp;
producing traditional Japanese playing cards.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:12.900
When long-time President Hiroshi Yamauchi&nbsp;
took over the family business in the 1940s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.900
he tried to figure out a way&nbsp;
to extend the company’s reach.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.260
He secured a partnership with&nbsp;
the Walt Disney Company to start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:22.020
creating Western style playing&nbsp;
cards with Disney characters.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:26.940
However, by the 1960s, people were no&nbsp;
longer interested in playing cards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:31.560
The Japanese gravitated toward other&nbsp;
hobbies like arcade games or bowling.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:35.640
In a desperate attempt to stay&nbsp;
afloat, Nintendo sold everything.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.000
Literally everything.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.640
Including a toy vacuum cleaner.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:47.580
Gunpei Yokoi joined Nintendo in 1965 when the&nbsp;
company wasn’t at all involved in video games.

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:52.920
He studied electronics at university and&nbsp;
watched his friends get plum engineering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:56.400
jobs straight out of school while he&nbsp;
struggled to land a job in his field.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.880
He was adamant that he wouldn’t&nbsp;
need to leave Kyoto to do it.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:03.660
It just so happened that Nintendo’s&nbsp;
headquarters were in Kyoto.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:07.800
Nintendo was never his first choice&nbsp;
or even second or third choice.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:13.440
But out of desperation, he applied. Not even to&nbsp;
work as an engineer, but as a maintenance staffer.

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:17.280
He was hired by Nintendo in 1965 to maintain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.980
the assembly-line machines that&nbsp;
manufactured the playing cards.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:23.640
He found he had a lot of time on his&nbsp;
hands and started playing around in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:25.740
the factory where he had access to the machinery.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:31.200
For his own amusement, he built an extendable&nbsp;
arm toy that could grip things. Kinda like this.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:35.640
That caught the attention of President&nbsp;
Yamauchi, who called Yokoi into his office.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:39.120
Yokoi thought he was going to&nbsp;
get chewed out by the boss.&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:42.960
But instead, the president was impressed&nbsp;
by his employee’s inventiveness.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:46.380
He asked him to make a toy that the&nbsp;
company could sell at Christmas.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:52.020
According to the book “Game Over” by David Sheff,&nbsp;
Yokoi asked the boss: ''What should I make?''

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:54.660
Yamauchi replied: “Something great”

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:58.320
He thought he could turn his&nbsp;
extended arm into a great product.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:03.660
The Ultra Hand was a simple plastic&nbsp;
zig-zag contraption that extended and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:06.480
contracted like an accordion when&nbsp;
you squeezed the handles together.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:10.140
It came with a set of three balls and&nbsp;
stands which could be used for practice.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:12.840
The Ultra Hand was a great success,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:17.160
Nintendo’s first product ever to&nbsp;
sell more than a million units.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:20.220
President Yamauchi then asked&nbsp;
Yokoi to work on more toys.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:24.780
He designed the Ultra Machine&nbsp;
which came out in 1967.

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:27.480
The ball shot out toward the&nbsp;
player who tried to hit it.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:30.180
It was a good idea to make&nbsp;
a toy related to baseball&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:32.940
considering how popular the sport is in Japan.

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:37.440
This was Nintendo’s second product&nbsp;
to sell over a million units.

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:42.960
Yokoi devised many more hits including the&nbsp;
“Love Tester” which supposedly measured the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:46.620
level of affection between a couple when&nbsp;
they held hands and touched the device.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:51.540
It was the first Nintendo product to run on&nbsp;
electricity, though it was quite rudimentary.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:54.120
The love test was yet another hit.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:59.820
But not everything Nintendo sold was successful.&nbsp;
One product nearly killed the company.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:04.320
In 1973, Nintendo came out with the Laser Clay.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:07.980
Yokoi had the idea of turning&nbsp;
abandoned bowling alleys into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:12.360
shooting galleries where people fire&nbsp;
at clay pigeons with light-beam guns.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:14.400
By then bowling was on the wane.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:19.440
Although this was a clever idea, it was&nbsp;
expensive and the timing was terrible.

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:22.860
The same year it came out, OPEC drastically raised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:26.100
the price of crude oil and&nbsp;
gasoline prices skyrocketed.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.800
Nobody was in the mood to spend money on toys.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:32.640
Nintendo plunged 5 billion yen in the red.&nbsp;
(or $36 million US in today’s dollars)

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:38.160
It narrowly escaped…but it&nbsp;
did…and then became even stronger.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:43.740
The bright side was that laser clay&nbsp;
paved the way for the beloved duck hunt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:46.680
the classic shooter video&nbsp;
game released a decade later.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:52.080
With the exception of Laser Clay, many&nbsp;
of Nintendo’s early products were a hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:59.280
within Japan but didn’t reach any level of global&nbsp;
success until…the release of Ten Billion in 1980.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:01.440
You had to get the colored&nbsp;
balls into their original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:04.380
position after they were rotated by two drums.

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:07.560
This was Nintendo’s response to the Rubik’s cube.

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:09.420
For many people around the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:13.200
Yokoi’s Ten Billion was the first&nbsp;
time they had heard of Nintendo.

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:18.060
As the company grew bigger, its research and&nbsp;
development division split into three units.

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:22.680
Yokoi was in charge of unit 1 which&nbsp;
focused on small, hand-held games.

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:26.880
The story goes that Yokoi was sitting&nbsp;
on a bullet train one day when he saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:31.500
a bored businessman fiddling around&nbsp;
with his calculator. Yokoi realized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:36.240
the appeal of portable games that anyone&nbsp;
could play anywhere to pass the time.

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:39.900
But he had to convince his boss that&nbsp;
this should be the company’s direction.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:43.920
One day, when President Yamauchi’s&nbsp;
chauffeur was off with the flu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:46.140
Yokoi drove the president to a meeting.

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:49.800
He took the opportunity to pitch&nbsp;
his idea of creating a portable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:53.520
gaming device but President Yamauchi&nbsp;
didn’t seem all that interested.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:59.040
However, as fate would have it, Yokoi dropped&nbsp;
Yamauchi off at a meeting where he sat beside the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:04.020
president of the world’s largest manufacturer of&nbsp;
calculators, Sharp, and mentioned the idea to him.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:08.700
Things took off from there, and Nintendo&nbsp;
decided to give portable games a shot.

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:12.060
In 1980, Nintendo released Game and Watch.

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:15.420
Although Game and Watch would go&nbsp;
through 60 different versions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:19.380
what remained the same was the&nbsp;
distinct cross-shaped control pad.

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:23.520
This would later end up on&nbsp;
Yokoi’s masterpiece, the Game Boy.

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:28.260
But I’m getting ahead of myself. We’re&nbsp;
still a few years away from the Game Boy.

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:31.740
Things were looking promising for&nbsp;
Nintendo in the early eighties.

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:39.060
However, starting in 1983, revenues for video&nbsp;
game makers dropped almost 97% in two years!!

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:43.980
Home video game revenues peaked&nbsp;
at around $3.2 billion in 1983,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:47.880
then fell to around $100 million by 1985.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:51.660
The industry-wide decline was&nbsp;
blamed on an oversaturated market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:55.500
especially in North America, where there&nbsp;
were too many consoles to choose from.

00:05:55.500 --> 00:06:02.280
President Yamauchi also attributed the crash to&nbsp;
poorly designed games. He felt market leader Atari&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:07.080
gave too much power to third-party developers&nbsp;
who produced “rubbish games”, in his words.

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:12.060
Atari’s E.T. is widely considered&nbsp;
the worst video game of all time.

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:17.700
The video game industry also had to fend off&nbsp;
competition from the rise of personal computers.

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:20.220
Store owners questioned whether video games&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:23.700
were just a passing fad that&nbsp;
would eventually flame out.

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:30.300
Then, in 1985, Nintendo released&nbsp;
the Family Computer - also known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:33.097
as the Nintendo Entertainment System (NES).

00:06:33.097 --> 00:06:36.180
President Yamauchi wanted a simple video game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:39.060
console and that’s exactly&nbsp;
what Nintendo’s offered.

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:41.220
It started running as soon as you turned it on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:44.940
and if there was a problem, all you&nbsp;
had to do was press the reset button.

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:47.940
To avoid Atari’s mistake of poorly designed games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:51.120
Nintendo strictly controlled&nbsp;
third-party development.

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:54.780
It introduced a lock-out chip&nbsp;
so that any unauthorized games&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:57.120
would be “locked out”, they couldn’t be played.

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:02.400
The company also decided that approved video&nbsp;
games would carry the official Nintendo seal.

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:07.920
Nintendo managed to win back the public’s trust&nbsp;
and the gaming industry started to recover.

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:14.760
By 1989, Nintendo dominated the market,&nbsp;
earning $2.5 billion in revenue a year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:19.140
and around 70% of North America’s&nbsp;
video game market share,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:23.880
largely thanks to the stunning&nbsp;
success of Super Mario Bros.

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:30.060
Yokoi would personally mentor the&nbsp;
game’s designer, Shigeru Miyamoto.

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:33.480
Yokoi convinced him to give&nbsp;
Mario superhuman qualities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:36.900
like the ability to fall from any height unharmed.

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:40.500
Super Mario Bros helped Nintendo&nbsp;
wipe out its competitors.

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:43.380
The mustachioed plumber with overalls and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:47.460
red cap was such a hit that Mario&nbsp;
became Nintendo’s official mascot.

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:51.240
Instead of sitting pretty with&nbsp;
the success of its console,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:57.240
Nintendo reinvented itself again when&nbsp;
Yokoi invented his greatest success.

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:03.540
The bulky grey brick won the hearts&nbsp;
of the world upon its release in 1989.

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:08.520
When Yokoi started to design the Game Boy, he&nbsp;
implemented a development philosophy called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:14.220
“Lateral thinking with withered technology”&nbsp;
which means innovative ideas can lead to new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:18.780
products using pre-existing technology... as&nbsp;
opposed to producing cutting-edge technology.

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:24.240
Yokoi’s goal was to build a product that&nbsp;
was inexpensive enough to mass produce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:29.220
so that it was affordable for consumers.&nbsp;
And - it also had to be fun, of course.

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:33.720
Nintendo decided to use a monochrome LCD screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.720 --> 00:08:36.960
instead of color because the&nbsp;
battery life lasted longer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:42.120
and it was easier to see under direct sunlight&nbsp;
which allowed people to enjoy playing outdoors.

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:47.880
It wasn’t perfect, though; the screen had a green&nbsp;
tint and the characters were always very blurry.

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:51.720
Yokoi agonized over that small&nbsp;
screen, and at one point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:56.340
was so stressed that he stopped eating&nbsp;
and was diagnosed with acute malnutrition.

00:08:56.340 --> 00:09:03.480
He recalled: "I considered it my biggest failure.&nbsp;
At one point, I honestly contemplated suicide."

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:10.140
Yet what he considered his biggest failure&nbsp;
turned out to be his greatest success.

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:15.540
What Yokoi didn’t realize then was that&nbsp;
people are okay with imperfections.

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:21.000
Kids didn’t really care about the specs. They&nbsp;
just wanted a fun, highly addictive portable game.

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:23.520
By the way, the big difference between the Game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:27.480
Boy and Game &amp; Watch is that Game&nbsp;
&amp; Watch could only play one game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:32.820
whereas you could swap out the cartridges on&nbsp;
the Game Boy and play whatever game you wished.

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:37.500
The Game Boy sold a million units&nbsp;
in the U.S. in just a few weeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:42.420
and would go on to sell nearly 120&nbsp;
million units in total worldwide.

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:49.200
So, when he left the company in 1996 to start&nbsp;
his own gaming company, people were shocked.

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:55.200
Japanese executives tended to stay at one company&nbsp;
for life, so some speculated that he was fired.

00:09:55.200 --> 00:10:00.562
Also, the follow-up to the game boy - the&nbsp;
virtual boy - was a commercial failure.

00:10:00.562 --> 00:10:00.627
It was a video game console that&nbsp;
used virtual reality technology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.627 --> 00:10:02.580
but it was a flop because it was&nbsp;
costly and uncomfortable to wear.

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:06.840
Yokoi was 55 years old when&nbsp;
he started Koto Laboratory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:11.160
which eventually released a handheld&nbsp;
gaming console called WonderSwan in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:14.340
partnership with Bandai (Band-dye) in&nbsp;
order to compete with the Game Boy.

00:10:14.340 --> 00:10:20.460
But Yokoi would never see WonderSwan&nbsp;
hit the market. Because…tragedy struck.

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:28.380
On October 4, 1997, he was a passenger in a car&nbsp;
that rear-ended a truck on an expressway about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:34.380
200 miles northwest of Tokyo. When Yokoi and the&nbsp;
driver, a business associate, got out to examine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:40.860
the damage, another car struck them, killing&nbsp;
Yokoi. His associate suffered a fractured rib.

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:44.760
Gunpei Yokoi was 56 years old.

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:49.200
His tombstone is inscribed with&nbsp;
the dates of his top creations.

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:56.100
One reason Yokoi was so successful is that he&nbsp;
had a deep understanding of what people desired.

